The only time I've ever seen Ultrix really munch filesystems was on a
785 with a bad memory controller, but that problem led to inodes rotting
in the buffer cache, not bad magic numbers.

You can get some really screwey super-block messages if you try to mix
4.3 Tahoe and Ultrix filesystems, but you seem to claim innocence with
this and the usual swap area blows super block problems.

The usual questions:  What release of Ultrix, what system, what controller,
what drive?  Next, I'd dump the superblock and see what's actually happening
to it...  When you get the bad magic on the alternate super-blocks, are
they really blown, or is it still complaining about the primary?  Are they
really lunched too?

When you say nothing else was touched, do you mean no other partitions were
used, or that they were used but seem to be ok?  Does chpt -i show that
you aren't using any overlapping partitions?

