In article <5554@umd5.umd.edu> jg13@umd5.umd.edu (Allon Stern) writes:
>I recently was given a DEC DF112 Modem, 1200 baud, I think.  Does anybody
>have any operation manuals for this beast?  I can get it to respond, but
>all it gives me is "error" every time I type a key when it isn't in a
>test mode.  Thanks.

This modem has an extremely limited command set.

    .  You must begin the command with CTRL/A or CTRL/B.  CTRL/A is for what
       they call "burst" mode.  I think it is supposed to indicate that the
       commands are coming from a computer rather than a human, and suppresses
       echo.  CTRL/B is for "interactive" mode.  I've never tried CTRL/A.  The
       df112 entry in /etc/acucap distributed with DEC's Ultrix specifies the
       "ss" value (synchronization string) as "^B".  This indicates that DEC
       thinks that tip and uucp should use "interactive" rather than "burst"
       mode.
    .  Optionally follow that with either "P" or "T" for pulse or tone dialing.
    .  Then type the digits that make up the telephone number.  The character
       "=" in this string indicates that the modem should pause for 15 seconds.
    .  The character "#" delimits the end of the phone number, and tells the
       modem to start dialing.
    .  The letters "A", "B", "C", or "D" may be used to terminate the phone
       number, in which case the phone number is stored under that name.  The
       number can be referenced by typing the letter where you would have typed
       the phone number.
    .  A stored phone number can be displayed by terminating the command with
       carriage return <CR> instead of "#".

That's about all it can do.

Here are some acucap entries for use with a df112.  df112db is identical
to df112, except that it has the debugging flag turned on.  Note that even
though the file format looks very much like the termcap file, the code which
parses acucap is not the same as is used for termcap.  Among other things,
it does not recognize the "tc" string.  If it did, df112db could have been
done as:
	df112db:db:tc=df112
Lacking the "tc" capability, it was necessary to make df112db a copy of df112.
Likewise for df112-p.

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Cut here - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
#
# Dec df112 modem. 40 second maximum for carrier detect.
#
#  ('ls' true because df112 doesn't need a full sec between characters)
#
#  (switches factory)
df112db|DF112db|DEC df112 debug mode:\
	:cr:hu:re:ls:ss=^B:sr=Ready\r\n:sd#250000:di=T:fd#50:\
	:dt=#:dd#50000:os=\r\nAttached\r\n:rs==:db:
df112|DF112|DEC df112:\
	:cr:hu:re:ls:ss=^B:sr=Ready\r\n:sd#250000:di=T:fd#50:\
	:dt=#:dd#50000:os=\r\nAttached\r\n:rs==:
#  (Pulse dialing) df112 (switches factory)
df112-p|DF112-P|DEC df112 with pulse dialing:\
	:cr:hu:re:ls:ss=^B:sr=Ready\r\n:sd#250000:di=P:fd#50:\
	:dt=#:dd#50000:os=\r\nAttached\r\n:rs==:
#
# Options used in this capabilities file are:
#
#      Strings which can be written to a modem:
#
# ss = Synchronization string. Initially output to modem to 'see' if its awake.
# sr = What modem should say to ss having been sent.
# di = Dial initialization string (ie start a dialing sequence).
# dt = How to finish a dial sequence (needed since some modems need
#      to put the number to be dialed between some string).
# dr = What modem should say if the dial went off correctly.
# cs = Completion string which is a catchall for sync/dial strings
#      (Sent out last but probably not used by many modems).
# ab = An abort string to be send to modem if we abort tip.
# ds = A sequence used when modem is disconnected.
# os = A string which says a remote system came online (after 
#      carrier is detected).
# rs = A single character replacement string for the default delay
#      character of '=' OR '-' (which is default for DEC equipment). This
#      conversion only takes place within the phone number string
#      passed in.
#
#      Logical flags:
#
# cr = Wait for carrier to be sensed.
# hu = hangup if line goes away.
# re = Reset dtr (turn it off then on) before dialing begins.
# rd = delay 1 second after toggling dtr. Only used if re flag is set.
# ls = use local sleep routine (rather than sleep) & thus all delays
#      must be given in microseconds (1000000 apx eq 1 second).
# si = ignore all response strings as this modem is attached to 
#	an interface which gives no characters back until carrier
#	is up (eg a dmf32).
#
#      Delays:
#
# sd = Synchronization delay (time between sync characters).
# dd = Dial delay (time between dial chars).
# da = Dial acknowledge delay (time to wait before looking for dial response)
# cd = Completion delay (time between completion chars).
# fd = Full delay (total time we wait for carrier detect).
#
#	Debug:
#
# db = Print info useful for debugging new entries.
- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

Good luck!
-- 
	Mark of the Valley of Roses
