Actually, to give you some history, we did the best we could in the time we
had.  Getting installation to work turns out to be one of the most time
consuming parts of doing a machine.  It was literally the pacing item for
good parts of the PMAX (DECstation 3100) project.

This is because to make a one line change in the installation script
takes a very long time; you have to build a new distribution kit, and
do the installation, and debug the remains.  Such a cycle takes hours;
it is not particularly hard work, but consumes many man hours and to be
done right requires someone who is meticulously careful.  And you get to
do it once for each supported machine configuration.  Adding an additional
disk type to test installation on other size and type devices would have 
greatly slowed this cycle.  There plain wasn't time.

It was hard enough to get things set for first release.  And since then
there has been the 5400, 5800, DS3100S, and other unannouced products,
as well as several VAXen that I no longer keep track of, to keep them
all out of mischief.  

So if things are not as graceful as they might be, have a bit of sympathy 
for the developers. In fact, they did as much as they could in the time 
available to make it possible for you to deprive Digital of revenue by 
attaching 3rd party disks (or if you look at it another way, to enhance 
Digital's revenue by making the machine more desirable since you can
attach 3rd party disks to it, thereby selling more machines).  To my
knowledge, it was not "mickey mouse crap" or "marketing tactics".
If it had been, you can be sure people could have and would easily have
made it very much harder, with no provisions to accomodate other people's
drives at all, much less posting of directions to the net on how to
make it work, as has been done.

				- Jim Gettys
				  


