In article <499@galadriel.bt.co.uk>, terry@galadriel.bt.co.uk (Terry MacSweeney) writes:
> I hope someone out there can help me:
> I've a VAXstation 3100 with an RX23 3 & 1/2 inch floppy drive. How can I
> format a disc so that I can copy files from my hard disc to it?
> Also what do I need to be able to use IBM compatible disks?

Depends on the floppy disks. If they are the (ruinously expensive) disks
from DEC, they are already formatted at the low level. Just use INITIALIZE
(with suitable parameters) to build an ODS-2 file system on them.

If you need to low-level format them (e.g. you bought 3M disks for example)
then VMS provides no way of doing that. Shut down VMS, and type the
following at the >>> prompt:

      TEST 70

You then get asked for a number for the disk drive; the answer is 2. Then
for confirmation (the answer is 1). Don't worry about what this might do
to the hard disk; this is a floppy-only procedure (TEST 75 does the hard
disk if I remember rightly).

Note that you need HD (high density) disks for this. Also note that despite
the occasional article to the contrary, INITIALIZE does *not* do the low
level format; those that say it does just happened to have preformatted
disks!

I don't know about the IBM PC compatibility question, but it isn't hard to
do. I believe someone said a program was being done for the next (?) DECUS
SIG tape.
